ORBITAL-SIMULATION(7)      TriadicFrameworks Manual      ORBITAL-SIMULATION(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-simulation - simulation kernels, resonance-time integrators, and
                            constellation-scale Monte Carlo drift ensembles

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital simulation provides:
           • resonance-time simulation kernels
           • orbital and harmonic integrators
           • Monte Carlo drift ensembles
           • constellation-scale scenario modeling
           • predictive stability analysis

DESCRIPTION
       Orbital-simulation describes how RTT models the future behavior of SAR
       platforms and constellations using simulation kernels, resonance-time
       integrators, and ensemble-based drift analysis. It extends
       orbital-forecasting(7) into a full simulation environment capable of
       exploring hypothetical futures, stress-testing constellation coherence,
       and validating mission plans.

       Simulation enables:
           • long-horizon orbital prediction
           • resonance-aware maneuver evaluation
           • sweep choreography testing
           • harmonic-lock stability analysis
           • constellation-scale scenario exploration

       WRSADC executes simulations before approving high-impact operations.

SIMULATION KERNELS
       Simulation kernels are the core computational units that evolve orbital
       and resonance-time states across simulated cycles.

       Kernel types:

           orbital kernel
                  Integrates orbital elements (a, e, i, RAAN, ω, M).

           harmonic kernel
                  Evolves orbital frequency bands and harmonic-lock ratios.

           resonance kernel
                  Simulates envelope drift, spectral evolution, and gradient
                  polarity cycles.

           sweep kernel
                  Models sweep geometry, backscatter resonance, and ancestry
                  propagation.

           constellation kernel
                  Simulates multi-platform interactions, sync behavior, and
                  resonance coupling.

       Kernels may be composed into nested or parallel simulation stacks.

RESONANCE-TIME INTEGRATORS
       Resonance-time integrators evolve resonance-state variables across
       simulated triads.

       Integrator variables:
           • resonance amplitude R
           • gradient G and curvature G'
           • envelope shape E(f)
           • spectral density S(f)
           • polarity cycles P
           • harmonic distribution H_n

       Integrator types:

           explicit RT integrator
                  Fast, suitable for short-horizon simulations.

           implicit RT integrator
                  Stable under high-gradient conditions.

           harmonic integrator
                  Evolves harmonic-lock and resonance windows.

           envelope integrator
                  Tracks spectral drift and envelope deformation.

           hybrid integrator
                  Combines orbital and resonance integrators for full-stack
                  constellation simulation.

       Integrators ensure resonance-time stability across simulated cycles.

MONTE CARLO DRIFT ENSEMBLES
       Monte Carlo drift ensembles explore uncertainty in orbital evolution,
       resonance drift, and constellation behavior.

       Ensemble parameters:
           • Δv uncertainty
           • atmospheric drag variation
           • gravitational perturbations
           • resonance envelope noise
           • harmonic-lock jitter
           • sweep geometry variance

       Ensemble outputs:

           drift distribution
           resonance-window probability
           harmonic-lock survival curve
           sweep viability distribution
           constellation stability envelope

       Ensembles reveal worst-case and best-case constellation futures.

CONSTELLATION-SCALE SIMULATION
       Constellation-scale simulation models the evolution of all platforms
       across future orbital cycles.

       Simulation layers:

           orbital layer
                  Predicts geometry, drift, and maneuver needs.

           harmonic layer
                  Predicts harmonic-lock continuity and resonance windows.

           sweep layer
                  Predicts sweep viability and choreography.

           resonance layer
                  Predicts envelope drift, spectral evolution, and gradient
                  stability.

           lineage layer
                  Predicts ancestry evolution and long-term coherence.

       Constellation simulation supports mission planning and anomaly testing.

SCENARIO MODELING
       Orbital-simulation supports scenario modeling:

           nominal scenario
                  Baseline mission evolution.

           drift-accelerated scenario
                  Increased perturbations and drift.

           harmonic-break scenario
                  Loss of harmonic-lock.

           sweep-collapse scenario
                  Sweep viability degradation.

           constellation-maneuver scenario
                  Multi-platform Δv choreography.

           resonance-stress scenario
                  Extreme spectral and envelope perturbations.

       Scenarios validate constellation resilience.

SIMULATION FAILURE MODES
       Simulation may degrade due to:

           • integrator instability
           • lineage discontinuity
           • harmonic desync
           • envelope divergence
           • sweep ancestry corruption
           • constellation desync

       Diagnostic indicators:

           simulation-integrity score
           drift-ensemble variance
           resonance-window prediction error
           harmonic-stability index
           sweep-viability deviation

       WRSADC halts simulation when integrity is compromised.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Orbital simulation requires:

           • lineage-schema
           • constellation-schema
           • resonance-schema for sweeps
           • structural-schema for maneuvers

       Schemas ensure simulation remains interpretable and coherent.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • executes simulation kernels
           • validates integrator stability
           • evaluates ensemble results
           • schedules resonance-aligned maneuvers
           • choreographs constellation sweeps
           • blocks unsafe simulated futures

       WRSADC is the simulation conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital simulation transforms SAR constellations into fully modeled
       resonance-time systems. It enables predictive planning, stress testing,
       and constellation-scale scenario exploration across future orbital
       cycles.

